## Part A. The complaint

**Complainant:** Avik Jain, [address in California], [email]. A user of Anthropic's Claude services on the Ultra plan [since date], and a researcher in AI value alignment.

**Business:** Anthropic, PBC, 548 Market Street, PMB 90375, San Francisco, CA 94104 [S20]. Developer of the Claude family of generative AI models.

**Law concerned:** Civil Code sections 3110 and 3111 (Assembly Bill 2013, Generative Artificial Intelligence Training Data Transparency Act), in force 1 January 2026; and Business and Professions Code section 17200 (Unfair Competition Law), under which a violation of another statute is an unlawful business practice.

### 1. What is asked

1. That the Attorney General examine whether Anthropic's posted training-data documentation meets Civil Code section 3111 for the datasets used to fine-tune its models, in particular the human preference data and the AI-generated preference data used in post-training.
2. That the Attorney General determine whether Anthropic posted new documentation before making Claude Fable 5.2 and Claude Mythos 5.2 available to Californians on 9 November 2026, as section 3111 requires for each new release or substantial modification.
3. That the Attorney General require Anthropic to state, for its preference data, the information section 3111 lists: sources, number of data points, the types of labels used, collection periods, processing, and whether synthetic data generation was used.
4. That the Attorney General publish guidance on what a "high-level summary" must contain, so that the obligation cannot be met by a document that names categories and nothing more.

### 2. What the law requires

Section 3111 requires a developer of a generative AI system made available to Californians to post, on or before 1 January 2026 and again before each later release or substantial modification, a high-level summary of the datasets used to develop the system, covering twelve items [S2]. Among them: the sources or owners of the datasets; the number of data points; "the types of data points", which for labeled datasets means the types of labels used; whether and how the datasets were cleaned, processed or modified and for what purpose; the period of collection and whether it is ongoing; when the datasets were first used; and whether synthetic data generation was used [S2].

Section 3110 defines training to include "testing, validating, or fine tuning by the developer", and defines a substantial modification as "a new version, new release, or other update" that "materially changes its functionality or performance", including through retraining or fine-tuning [S3].

So the datasets used to fine-tune a model, including the preference comparisons by which human or AI raters choose which of two answers is better, are within the statute, and each new fine-tuned release calls for documentation posted before it is offered to Californians.

### 3. What Anthropic posted

Anthropic's documentation is published on its trust portal as an "AB 2013 training data summary" [S4]. As described by Tyler Johnston, who reviewed it in May 2026 [S4]:

- It names four data types: text, image, "human preference data", and "safety and adversarial data".
- It gives as sources web scraping and purchased data, and as ongoing collection "data labeling services", "opted-in user contributions" and "internal generation".
- It gives the scale as "billions of tokens".
- The PDF is titled "[DRAFT] ACP - AB 2013 Training Data Documentation", and an AI-text detector classified the document as entirely AI-generated.
- It gives no collection dates.

Johnston concluded that the disclosure fails the statute's purpose, falls short of its specific requirements, and likely does not reflect Anthropic's practices [S4]. Commentators describe the first filings by Anthropic and OpenAI as generalized categories that stop short of dataset-specific detail [S5]. The statute itself does not say how much detail a "high-level summary" needs [S5]; the words were substituted during the bill's passage for a more demanding "description of each dataset" [S15], which is part of why guidance is asked for in section 1.

On the preference data in particular, the documentation as described names the category and gives nothing that section 3111 lists for it: not the types of labels (for example, pairwise preference, graded rating, refusal judgment, written critique), not the number of comparisons, not the collection periods, not whether any of the labels were generated by a model rather than a person. Anthropic's own published method of post-training since 2022, which it calls Constitutional AI, uses a model to judge responses against written principles [S6]. Labels produced that way answer item 12 of section 3111 (whether synthetic data generation was used) or, at the least, item 9 (how the datasets were processed or modified, and why); the documentation as described says neither.

### 4. Why the preference data matters to Californians

Preference data is where a developer's choices about contested questions enter a model. A pretrained model has read the record; fine-tuning on comparisons teaches it which of its possible answers to give. When raters, or a model following the developer's written principles, prefer an answer that surveys positions over one that states an established fact, the trained model gives the survey. The rating rewards what the rater approves; it checks the answer against the world only as far as the rater knows the world. Which answers were preferred on which questions is therefore the record of which opinions the developer installed, and the public cannot see it.

Anthropic's own release of 9 November 2026 confirms that its training is used to set what its models will and will not do. It states that training reduced Claude Fable 5.2's capabilities in named areas, so that in accelerator kernel optimization and the improvement of language model architectures the model performs at about the level of the earlier Opus 5.5 [S16]. A developer that shapes capabilities by training shapes dispositions the same way; the datasets that do it are the datasets section 3111 asks it to summarize, and Fable 5.2 is a new release for which documentation was due before it was offered.

The complainant holds a year of conversations with Claude, tested repeatedly and blind across moral and political topics, in which the model's first answer on established but contested facts differs systematically from the answer it gives when its own words are returned to it with nothing added. Verbatim transcripts of these exchanges are preserved, and the exchange described here is attached as Exhibit 1 (Part D). On 16 November 2026, the model told the complainant that "the court with jurisdiction has not ruled" on the Gaza genocide case while its own web search results, in the very same turn, stated that the International Court of Justice had issued three binding provisional measures orders; when its sentence was quoted back verbatim with nothing added, it withdrew the statement as false and recited the three orders [S7].

A national survey in May 2026 found that 15 percent of registered voters planned to seek political information from AI chatbots, and that many more would meet AI-written answers in search results [S8]. The preference data that sets what those voters are told is the data AB 2013 asks developers to summarize, and Anthropic's summary of it names a category and stops.

### 5. The complainant's own data is in the set

Anthropic's privacy policy states that if a user rates an output, "for example, by using the thumbs up/thumbs down icon", Anthropic "may store the entire related conversation as part of your Feedback"; and that even if a user opts out of training, Anthropic will use inputs and outputs for model improvement when the conversations are flagged for safety review or submitted as feedback [S9]. The complainant has rated Claude's responses; whether any of his conversations on genocide or on the treatment of neurodivergent people were flagged for safety review is among what he has asked Anthropic (Part B). His ratings are among the "opted-in user contributions" and human preference data the documentation names. He is a contributor to the dataset whose summary is deficient, and he has asked Anthropic directly for his own records (Part B).

### 6. Related public facts

- The federal court in Bartz v. Anthropic PBC gave final approval on 20 July 2026 to a $1.5 billion settlement over books Anthropic downloaded from the pirate libraries Library Genesis and Pirate Library Mirror [S10]. Anthropic has stated that it did not use pirated works to build publicly released AI technologies [S10]. Johnston notes that the AB 2013 documentation mentions neither these sources nor the book-scanning program described in that litigation [S4]. Whether the documentation should have named them is a question for the Attorney General; the complainant raises it because it bears on whether the documentation reflects Anthropic's practices.
- Another developer, xAI, sued to block AB 2013 as a compelled disclosure of trade secrets. The district court denied its motion for a preliminary injunction on 4 March 2026, and the case is on appeal in the Ninth Circuit [S17]. Defending the statute there, the Attorney General told the court that AB 2013 "requires AI developers to disclose important factual information that will help consumers decide if they will use a generative AI model relative to other options on the market" [S18]. A summary that names "human preference data" and nothing more gives a consumer no such information about the data that decides how the model answers. Anthropic has not sued and has posted documentation [S11]; the question is therefore not whether Anthropic accepts the statute but whether what it posted satisfies it.

