## Part C. Statutory amendment for full preference-data transparency

Part A asks the Attorney General to enforce Civil Code section 3111 as enacted. Full democratic accountability requires more than a summary: every datapoint of post-training preference feedback should be public, as freedom of information over the normative judgments installed into systems consulted by voters. Proposed Civil Code section 3111.5:

> **3111.5.** (a) For each generative artificial intelligence system or service subject to section
> 3111 that is offered to the public for answering questions of fact or of public affairs, the
> developer shall publish, on or before the date the system or service or a substantial modification
> of it is made available to Californians, the following, in a machine-readable form that may be
> downloaded without charge:
>
> (1) Every preference record used to train the system or service, where a preference record is any
> comparison, rating, ranking or critique of one or more outputs, by a person or by an automated
> system, used to adjust the system's behavior. Each record shall include the input, each output
> compared, the preference or rating given, whether a person or an automated system gave it, and the
> version of the instructions under which it was given.
>
> (2) Every version of the instructions, rubrics, principles or constitutions given to persons or
> automated systems producing preference records, with the dates each was in use.
>
> (3) For persons producing preference records, the method by which they were recruited and the
> instructions given to them, with each person identified only by a pseudonymous identifier.
>
> (4) Every set of inputs and target outputs used to train the system to decline, defer, or answer
> in a prescribed form.
>
> (b) A developer shall remove from records published under subdivision (a) personal information as
> defined in section 1798.140 and information that would identify a person who produced a record.
> Nothing in this section requires the publication of model weights or source code.
>
> (c) The Legislature finds that the records described in subdivision (a) determine what information
> the public receives from these systems on matters of fact and public affairs, and that the public
> interest in that information outweighs any interest in keeping it secret.

Subdivision (c) addresses the trade-secret and compelled-speech challenges raised against AB 2013 in *X.AI LLC v. Bonta* [S17]; the Attorney General's own defense — that the statute compels factual disclosure consumers need to evaluate competing AI systems [S18] — grounds full preference-record disclosure directly.

